The  ability  to boot a system over the network is useful for two
kinds of systems: a system with no attached mass storage media to
boot or run from a system with a hard drive  that  only  contains
system  and  application  software, and user data is mounted over
the network from a central server.  It can also be done as a tem‐
porary measure while repairing or re‐installing file systems on a
local disk.  This capability is  necessarily  platform  dependent
because  of  its  dependence  on system firmware support; not all
platforms supported by are capable of being network booted.   The
protocols  used  to obtain a network address include, but are not
limited to: Reverse Address Resolution Protocol Dynamic Host Con‐
figuration Protocol Bootstrap Protocol This information can  also
be  derived  from non‐volatile or by a transform of a network in‐
terface address.  The protocols used to load a kernel over a net‐
work include, but are not limited to: Trivial File Transfer  Pro‐
tocol Network File System Remote Maintenance Protocol Maintenance
Operations  Protocol  Derivation of the filename of the secondary
